  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Windy conditions persist into tonight ahead of high \npressure returning this weekend.\n\nHigh pressure situated to our west will continue tracking eastward \ninto tomorrow, but in the meantime, we find ourselves under a tight \npressure gradient between this high and a low over Nova Scotia. This \ngradient is not expected to relax until around midday tomorrow, \nwhich will lead to continued breezy conditions tonight. Gusts to 30 \nmph are possible, especially towards the coasts, through tonight \nbased on the latest guidance. Further inland, these will be a bit \nweaker. Lows tonight will be noticeably colder compared to last \nnight following today's cold front and its associated CAA, falling \ninto the 30s across the region. Dry conditions continue as well, \nwith dew points falling into the 30s and 20s through the rest of \nthis afternoon into tonight.\n\nA cooler day is expected Saturday as well due to the aforementioned \nCAA post-front. 925 mb temperatures dip to around 0C by tomorrow \nmorning (compared to today's 10C temperatures at that level), and NW \nwinds continue, yet weaken with high pressure building in. Highs on \nHalloween will only reach the 50s as a result. Skies clear, and \nwinds falling to or near calm Halloween night will allow for \nradiational cooling, dropping lows to the 20s and low 30s for much \nof the region. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...High pressure maintains quiet weather through \nat least Thursday with warmer than average highs in the 60s.\n\nHigh pressure will remain firmly in control of southern New \nEngland's weather clear through at least mid to late week as this \nstretch of pleasant weather continues. A large high moving in from \nNew York will initially move overhead on Sunday, replaced by another \ndropping south from Canada into Maine mid week. A weak trough will \npass over the Canadian maritimes around Tuesday bringing the \npotential for some showers to northern New England but lack of \nmoisture and dynamics over SNE will keep us dry. The next chance for \na pattern change comes around late Thursday/Friday when a mid level \ntrough may dig into the mid-Atlantic, but that is not a certainty at \nthis point. EPS and GEFS ensembles paint a 70-90% chance of \nmeasurable rain by Friday. In the mean time, high pressure maintains \na milder than average airmass overhead leading to comfortable highs \nin the 60s all week. Winds should be light until Friday when our \npassing low would bring a period of gusty winds.",
